---
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import "./dunstkreis.css";
---

<figure class="scene-frame wide" id="lighting">
    <div class="sky-view">
        <canvas data-field="canvas" aria-label="Four solids lit by the sky and the sun at the chosen moment and place"></canvas>
        <div class="sky-overlay">
            <span class="note" data-field="info"></span>
            <span class="steps" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Background" title="Behind the solids: the atmosphere rendered live for every pixel, or the sky map the lighting comes from">
                {
                    ["atmosphere", "sky map"].map((value) => (
                        <label>
                            <input type="radio" name="lighting-background" value={value} checked={value === "atmosphere"} />
                            <span>{value}</span>
                        </label>
                    ))
                }
            </span>
            <span class="steps" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Shadow rays over the sun disc" title="Shadow rays over the sun disc: one to its center for hard shadows, or 8 or 64 spread over it for soft ones">
                {
                    [
                        ["1", "hard"],
                        ["8", "8"],
                        ["64", "64"],
                    ].map(([value, label]) => (
                        <label>
                            <input type="radio" name="lighting-shadows" value={value} checked={value === "8"} />
                            <span>{label}</span>
                        </label>
                    ))
                }
            </span>
            <span class="sky-toggles">
                <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="sunLight" aria-pressed="true" title="Light the solids by the sun directly, with its shadows">sunlight</button>
                <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="skyLight" aria-pressed="true" title="Light the solids by the sky, image-based, from the nine coefficients of the sky map">sky light</button>
                <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="rotate" aria-pressed="false" title="Turn the solids slowly">rotate</button>
                <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="sunDisc" aria-pressed="true" title="Draw the sun disc in the background">sun disc</button>
                <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="occlusion" aria-pressed="true" title="Dim the sky's light where the solids hide the sky, by sixteen rays per pixel">occlusion</button>
            </span>
        </div>
        <p class="vis-credit footnote">Drag to turn around, scroll to move closer or far out. Raytraced with WebGPU.</p>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        A tetrahedron, a cube, an icosahedron and a sphere, lit by the sky above: the sun as a light of its own, with its
        shadows, soft from rays spread over its disc, and the sky's light from its nine coefficients, dimmed where the
        solids hide the sky. Raytraced, not a triangle in it, four rays per pixel,
        with the lighting refined over 64 frames while nothing moves. Every surface is
        white but the ground, a darker gray; the colors are the light's. Exposed like the sky.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./lighting.js";
</script>
